  "notes": [
    "The GT dataset does not contain raw control/treatment means or SDs - only the pre-computed effect sizes (E-A)/A and ln(r) are available.",
    "The primary effect size column in GT is 'ln(r)' (log response ratio), which corresponds to 'ln_rr' in the extraction data.",
    "The GT also has '(E-A)/A' which is the proportional change (equivalent to effect_pct/100 in extraction), not percent change.",
    "The extraction field 'effect_pct' is percent change; to compare with GT '(E-A)/A', divide effect_pct by 100.",
    "The 'Reference' column in GT appears to contain author+year strings used as study identifiers.",
    "Study mapping is approximate based on author surname and year from paper_id; exact GT Reference strings may differ slightly.",
    "The 'n' column in GT appears to be sample size per observation, matching extraction 'n' field.",
    "No raw means/variances are stored in GT, so validation must rely on the pre-computed ln(r) or (E-A)/A columns.",
    "The extraction data contains many moderator fields not present in GT columns, which are used for subgroup analysis but not for direct GT matching.",
    "Paper 037_de_2000 has an unusual author prefix 'de' which may correspond to a 'de Graaff', 'de Pury', or similar author in GT.",
    "Paper 058_ONeill_1987 uses an apostrophe-free version of O'Neill; GT Reference may use O'Neill 1987.",
    "Unit diversity in extraction data is high (mg/g, %, mg/kg, etc.) but GT only stores the ratio/log-ratio, so unit mismatches do not affect effect size comparison.",
    "The GT 'Study Type' values match extraction study_type values exactly.",
    "The GT 'Element' values match extraction element values exactly.",
    "The GT 'Tissue' values match extraction tissue values exactly."
  ]
